=== L2 Characters: Rani, Abdul, Hari, Lascar and Queen ===
id: eeef78c2-aba4-41d4-8873-9bce93ad28f4
slug: characters-rani-abdul-hari-lascar-and-queen
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:56:01.090947+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 25

--- DESCRIPTION ---
An ayah, the Queen's munshi, an Indian MP, a Lascar sailor and the British figures whose worlds they enter.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">How Character Works in <em>The Empress</em></h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Tanika Gupta builds her characters on two axes: historical ground (some are real people; some are imaginative reconstructions of documented communities) and dramatic function (each character serves a specific structural and thematic purpose). As a performer or designer approaching this play, your task is to translate character motivation into concrete, physical choices: how does Rani hold her body the moment the English family leaves the docks without her? How does Abdul Karim&rsquo;s <dfn class="term" data-def="The physical posture, bearing and quality of movement a performer uses to embody a character's status and inner life.">physical bearing</dfn> change between his early months of nervous service and his later confidence as Victoria&rsquo;s munshi?</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n3">Rani: The Invented Protagonist</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n4">Rani is the play&rsquo;s moral centre. She has no historical name because the ayahs whose lives Gupta draws on largely went unrecorded, but the research behind her is real. Her character arc moves from abandonment and isolation through community &mdash; the Lascar sailors, the Ayahs&rsquo; Home &mdash; to a moment of solidarity that gives the play its emotional resolution. As a performer playing Rani, you might consider how her <dfn class="term" data-def="The physical distance between performers on stage, used deliberately to express the emotional or power relationship between characters.">proxemics</dfn> shift across the play: isolated at the docks, downstage centre and physically contracted; in the Ayahs&rsquo; Home, beginning to expand into shared space with other women.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n5">In terms of vocal choices, Rani&rsquo;s language moves between formal English &mdash; the register she has learned in service &mdash; and her own Bengali identity. A performer might use a careful, slightly flattened English accent when addressing English characters, shifting in pitch and pace when she speaks to Lascar Mr Das or in moments of private emotion. This code-switching is itself a character choice: it tells the audience that Rani has learned to perform a certain identity for English employers, and the release from that performance is where her true character lives.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n6" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name three specific performer choices you could make for Rani in the docks abandonment scene &mdash; one vocal, one physical and one related to proxemics.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Rani is an invented protagonist grounded in the real history of ayahs stranded in Victorian London. Her character journey moves from isolation to community to solidarity. Examiners reward performers who can specify her vocal register shift and physical transformation across the play.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Abdul Karim: History on Stage</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Abdul Karim is a historical figure and Gupta stays close to the documented record: he arrived at Windsor in 1887, became Victoria&rsquo;s Indian secretary, taught her Hindustani, was resented by the court and had his papers burned by the royal household after her death. As a dramatic character, his arc is one of extraordinary ascent and ultimate erasure. A performer playing Abdul must hold these two things simultaneously: the personal warmth and intelligence of the man, and the knowledge that his story was deliberately destroyed after Victoria died.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n9">In the court scenes, Abdul&rsquo;s physical choices should communicate his dual status: the formal upright posture and deliberate, measured movements of a man who understands he is under constant scrutiny; and underneath that, the private dignity of someone with a strong sense of his own worth. <dfn class="term" data-def="A term from Brecht's theatre: a social gesture or attitude embodied in a specific physical action, revealing a character's class, status or relationship to power.">Gestus</dfn> is a useful concept here. Abdul&rsquo;s gestus in the court scenes might be understood as a controlled stillness &mdash; the conscious management of self-presentation under an oppressive gaze.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Queen Victoria: Warmth within the System</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Gupta&rsquo;s Victoria is one of the play&rsquo;s most nuanced characters. She is genuinely fond of Abdul, genuinely curious about India, and uses what power she has to protect him. But she is also the Empress of India &mdash; the figurehead of the system that abandons Rani at Tilbury and burns Abdul&rsquo;s papers. A director must resist letting the audience simply like Victoria. The most interesting staging of Victoria keeps both of these truths visible at once. One design approach: a golden-yellow <dfn class="term" data-def="A lighting effect using a shaped template (gobo) slotted into a lantern to project a pattern of light onto the stage, used to create atmosphere or symbolic images.">gobo</dfn> pattern suggesting the rays of empire in her scenes at court, beautiful but also inescapable &mdash; the very light that flatters her power casts shadows on those around her.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Is Hari Mohan Lal the same person as Dadabhai Naoroji?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">Hari Mohan Lal is a dramatic composite figure inspired by the real Dadabhai Naoroji, who was elected Liberal MP for Finsbury Central in 1892 &mdash; the first South Asian MP in British parliamentary history. Gupta draws on that real political history without making Hari identical to Naoroji. Examiners reward students who understand this as a dramaturgical choice: Gupta uses Hari to represent the emerging South Asian political voice in Britain without the legal and narrative constraints of a purely biographical depiction. This is the same technique she uses with Rani.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Hari Mohan Lal: The Political Voice</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Hari represents the nascent South Asian political presence in Westminster &mdash; the first generation of Indian-born men attempting to use the tools of British democracy to argue for Indian rights. As a dramatic character he occupies a different register from Rani or Abdul: more formal in language, more conscious of being observed and judged by a hostile political world. His scenes offer the play&rsquo;s most direct engagement with political argument, as opposed to personal experience. For a costume designer, Hari&rsquo;s dress is a significant choice: Western tailoring that signals his assimilation into the political world, versus any remaining South Asian detail in fabric or accessory that suggests his retained identity.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Lascar Mr Das and the East End Community</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">The Lascar sailor characters &mdash; Mr Das and others &mdash; represent a community that was physically present in Victorian East London for generations. They are among the most settled of the play&rsquo;s Indian figures: they have lodging houses, community rituals and networks of mutual support. A designer staging the Lascar lodgings might choose a set made from found maritime objects &mdash; rope, canvas, battered sea-chests &mdash; to communicate both the nautical origin of this community and the resourcefulness of people who have built a home from whatever they could carry. Warm, low-level <dfn class="term" data-def="Light that illuminates a small defined area of the stage, often used for intimate or symbolic moments.">lighting specials</dfn> could suggest lamplight and enclosure: a hidden world behind the docks.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">The English Antagonists: Lord Salisbury, Bertie and Mrs Wormwood</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">The English antagonist figures are not cartoons of villainy. Lord Salisbury and the Prince of Wales are motivated by a genuine belief in hierarchical order: for them, Abdul&rsquo;s elevation is not merely an affront to taste but a threat to the system that underpins their world. Mrs Wormwood, the English mistress who pays Rani off at the docks, may not even understand herself as doing harm. A director might use <dfn class="term" data-def="The planned arrangement of performers&rsquo; relative positions and distances to communicate status, power or emotional relationships.">proxemics</dfn> to communicate these power dynamics spatially: in the docks scene, Mrs Wormwood placed upstage, elevated on a trunk or step, with Rani standing downstage left, physically smaller in the frame, reinforces the power asymmetry before a word is spoken.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n19" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name one specific design choice you could make for the Lascar lodgings scene and explain what it communicates about that community.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The English antagonists &mdash; Lord Salisbury, Bertie, Mrs Wormwood &mdash; are more effective dramatically when staged as people defending a system they believe in, rather than as simple villains. This makes the play&rsquo;s argument about Empire more unsettling and more honest.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Performer&rsquo;s approach: playing Abdul Karim through Brecht and Stanislavski</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">The most sophisticated performer approach to Abdul Karim holds two traditions in tension. Stanislavski&rsquo;s method asks: what is Abdul&rsquo;s specific inner life at each moment? What does he want from Victoria in their Hindustani lessons? What does he feel when the court conspires against him? This psychological specificity keeps him real and sympathetic. Brecht&rsquo;s method asks: what is Abdul&rsquo;s social <em>gestus</em>? What does his physical presence tell the audience about the relationship between an Indian man and an Imperial court? Both questions matter. A great performance of Abdul does not choose between them: it lets the intimate Stanislavskian truth carry the Brechtian political argument without forcing it.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Rani is the play&rsquo;s invented protagonist grounded in the real history of ayahs; her character arc moves from isolation to solidarity and her vocal code-switching between English and Bengali registers is a key dramatic tool.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Abdul Karim and Queen Victoria are historical figures; Gupta&rsquo;s dramatic treatment of them holds personal warmth and Imperial complicity in tension simultaneously.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Staging character relationships through proxemics &mdash; placing characters in relation to each other on stage &mdash; communicates power, status and emotional truth before any dialogue is spoken.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n21">For an 8-mark performer question, the most common error is writing about what the character feels without translating that feeling into specific vocal and physical choices. Do not write &ldquo;I would play Rani as sad and frightened&rdquo; &mdash; instead write: &ldquo;I would play Rani with a low, controlled volume in the docks scene, a slight tightening of the jaw and a stillness of the hands that reads as suppressed panic, her gaze tracking the departing English family while her body remains rigid rather than reaching after them.&rdquo; Every emotion must become a named vocal quality and a named physical action anchored to a specific moment of the play.</p>

