  "text": "Analyse Gupta's intentions in using bilingual exchanges between Abdul and Victoria in the Hindustani lesson scenes. What does she want the audience to understand about language, power and identity at these moments?",
  "type": "12 marks — Analyse Intentions",
  "marks": "Mastering (10–12): Detailed analysis of Gupta's multiple intentions: to dramatise a genuine crossing of cultural barriers (Victoria choosing to learn Hindustani signals personal respect that transgresses Imperial norms); to make the audience feel the political charge of language as power (what does it mean that the Empress of India does not speak the language of India until a servant teaches her?); to reveal the court's hostility to Victoria's Indian connection as a form of racial policing. Candidate links each intention to a specific dramatic method (the scenes' intimate staging, the shift in tone from formal court English to the informality of a lesson, the sound of Hindustani on an English stage) and explains the audience effect. Substantiated judgement.\n\nSecure (7–9): Two intentions clearly articulated with some reference to dramatic method; audience effect identified though not fully developed for both.\n\nDeveloping (4–6): One intention identified with limited reference to how Gupta achieves it; audience effect implied.\n\nEmerging (1–3): Describes what happens in the Hindustani lesson scene rather than analysing Gupta's purpose."
 },
 {
  "text": "You are directing the scenes in The Empress set in the Ayahs' Home in Hackney, which show Rani building community with other stranded Indian women. How would you stage these scenes to communicate the theme of solidarity to the audience? Refer to set design, lighting, sound, costume and at least one performer's choices.",
  "type": "20 marks — Extended Staging Response",
  "marks": "Mastering (17–20): Specific, justified choices across all five areas. Set design: a configuration that places the performers in a community arrangement (circle or modified thrust) using domestic objects from India as props-as-symbols (worn sari cloth, cooking vessels, a child's toy). Lighting: a warm Fresnel wash in amber distinguishable from the Windsor gold by its lower intensity and informality, suggesting lamplight rather than opulence. Sound: Hindustani or Bengali song as underscore, transitioning to live voice when the community sings together, the acoustic shift from amplified to live marking a move from isolation to belonging. Costume: the worn, travel-stained garments of the ayahs carrying both the beauty of their Indian fabrics and the signs of a long journey. Performer (Rani): the specific physical unclenching of her body as she enters the community — hands opening, breath deepening, posture widening from the contracted docks silhouette — and the vocal shift from guarded English register to free Bengali. All choices justified with reference to the solidarity theme and the intended audience effect.\n\nSecure (11–16): Most design areas covered with concrete choices; at least one named performer choice; effect on audience identified.\n\nDeveloping (6–10): Some relevant ideas but generic; limited justification; one or more areas missing.\n\nEmerging (1–5): Basic listing; no named performer choices; descriptive."
 }
]

