  "marks": "Mastering (10–12): Detailed analysis of at least three distinct intentions, each linked to a specific dramatic method: the Jubilee as ironic framing (Gupta wants the audience to feel the gap between official Imperial celebration and individual human cost); the choice to place Rani's abandonment in the same year as Abdul's arrival (the two characters are simultaneously products of the same Imperial system, one ascending and one discarded); the use of real institutions (the Ayahs' Home, the Lascar community) to give the play historical authority and make the audience feel they are being shown something true, not invented. Each intention linked to specific staging/dialogue/structural method and the intended audience effect. Substantiated judgement.\n\nSecure (7–9): Two intentions clearly articulated with some reference to dramatic method and audience effect.\n\nDeveloping (4–6): One intention identified; limited reference to method; audience effect implied.\n\nEmerging (1–3): Describes the historical setting rather than analysing Gupta's dramaturgical purpose."
 },
 {
  "text": "You are designing the production of The Empress for a large proscenium arch theatre. How would you use set design, lighting, sound and costume to communicate the contrast between the Golden Jubilee world of Windsor and the hidden world of the Lascar community and the Ayahs' Home to the audience? Refer to specific design choices for both worlds.",
  "type": "20 marks — Extended Staging Response",
  "marks": "Mastering (17–20): Specific, justified choices across all four design disciplines applied to both worlds, with clear contrast between them. Windsor set design: raised levels, heavy drape or gauze backcloth in crimson and gold, a formal chair or dais for Victoria, sightlines that place the court in a hierarchy legible from every seat. Lascar/Ayahs' Home set design: lower, warmer space at stage level, maritime and domestic objects as props-as-symbols, no raised platform. Windsor lighting: deep gold and amber wash with a profile lantern special on Victoria's seat; profile lanterns for the court to create a sense of official illumination. East End lighting: warm low-level Fresnel wash, no specials, the suggestion of lamplight rather than stage light. Windsor sound: minimal, perhaps a formal orchestral underscore hinting at Victorian ceremony. East End sound: a diegetic soundscape with Bengali or Hindustani song from within the scene. Windsor costume: deep crimson and gilt for Victoria, black formal English tailoring for the court, Abdul's court livery marking him as different. Ayahs' Home costume: worn sari fabrics in blues and greens, suggesting the beauty of original garments after a long journey. All choices justified with reference to the historical context and the intended audience effect. Substantiated directorial judgement about how the contrast communicates the play's argument.\n\nSecure (11–16): Most design areas covered for both worlds with concrete choices; contrast between the two worlds clearly established; effect on audience identified.\n\nDeveloping (6–10): Some relevant choices but one or more areas missing or generic; contrast between worlds present but underdeveloped.\n\nEmerging (1–5): Basic listing of choices with minimal justification; little sense of the designed contrast between worlds."
 }
]

